FLOATING POINT: math.l vs math881.l  --  READ IF ACCURACY MATTERS=================================================================Microware ships two math libraries and the choice is NOT cosmetic.    -l=/dd/LIB/math.l      software floating point.  Works on any 68k.    -l=/dd/LIB/math881.l   uses the 68881/68882 FPU.  Needs one.MEASURED, on this emulator, same source, same compiler, only the librarychanged:                        math.l              math881.l         true value    sqrt(2)     1.4142135624        1.4142135624      1.4142135624    exp(1)      2.7182831246        2.7182818285      2.7182818285    sin(1)      0.8414709848 *      0.8414709848      0.8414709848    pow(2,10)   1024.0039           1024.0000         1024    H(100)      5.1873775189        5.1873775176      5.1873775176    * sin printed 0.8414711041 under math.lSo math.l gives roughly SINGLE precision for the transcendentals -- exp, sinand pow are wrong in the 6th to 7th significant digit, and pow(2,10), whichought to be exact, is off by four thousandths.  sqrt is exact in both, so aquick sqrt test will NOT reveal this.This is a property of the library, not of the emulator.  os9exec's floatingpoint is fine: with math881.l every value above is exact to ten digits.WHAT THIS MEANS FOR THIS DISKNearly everything here was built with -l=/dd/LIB/math.l, because that is thebuild that runs on any 68k with or without a coprocessor.  For most of theseprograms it does not matter.  For a few it might:    ephem, ephem881   astronomical ephemeris  (ephem881 is the 68881 build)    orbit, nasa       satellite orbits and orbital elements    pdraw             plotting    mtst, spline      curve fitting    rtf, for          the FORTRAN compiler and its runtime    paranoia, savage  floating-point ACCURACY benchmarks -- these exist                      precisely to measure this, and are worth runningIf you are doing anything where the seventh digit matters, rebuild from/dd/SRC with -l=/dd/LIB/math881.l instead, and run it on a machine with a68881/68882.  The sources are all here for exactly this reason.